Round-4 responses show persistent split: ~12 colleagues (majority) reaffirm C, citing explicit adult-zero-survival phrasing (distinct from larval-arrest language) as decisive evidence that maternal preload suffices for eclosion on bulk cholestanol alone (scenario 1 yields adults that then die), while scenario 2 supplies neither reserve nor threshold cholesterol (blocks eclosion). B minority (~6 colleagues) argues finite preload dilutes across larval instars/tissue growth, leaving late-pupal ecdysone surge unsupported and causing uniform pre-eclosion arrest; they claim adult-survival datum is compatible with pupal block once timing is considered.

Core evidence still favors C over B: adult-survival wording would be superfluous/redundant if arrest were always pre-eclosion. Dilution kinetics and exact pupal-surge timing remain unresolved but do not override problem phrasing. Current lean: C (78 %).}

